# LF in the repo AND in the working tree, so a recorded sha256 verifies on any OS.
# This repo has already quoted two hashes computed on different bases, and its one
# manifest pin never matched the file it pinned.
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
# *.py joined the list after @Stratogain noticed the patterns did not cover it
# (DanceNitra/ramr#5). Nothing pinned today is a .py file -- verify_manifest.py names nine
# .jsonl artifacts and nothing else -- so this closes a gap rather than fixing a break, and
# it rewrites nothing: all 48 tracked .py files are already stored with LF in HEAD, measured
# before the line was added.
*.py text eol=lf
# WHAT THIS PROMISE COVERS, after @Stratogain measured the edge on DanceNitra/ramr#3: the
# first sentence is true of checkouts made AFTER this line. A clone that predates it keeps
# CRLF in its working tree and git stays quiet about it, because it normalises on compare --
# so `git status` is clean while the file on disk and its blob hash differently. He measured
# exactly that on his clone (memaudit.py disk de8aa216 vs blob ea30350c); on a clone made
# after the line, disk and blob agree, which is what we see here. Nothing pins a .py today,
# so nothing breaks either way. The day something does, the fix for an older clone is a
# RE-CHECKOUT of the file, not a pull.
